# Universe content · elements (all 118)
# researched: IUPAC periodic table (atomic numbers, symbols, names, groups); standard states at room temperature;
#   natural vs lab-made from standard chemistry references; mass-number: the most common (or, for elements with no
#   stable form, the longest-lived) isotope, so protons = number and neutrons = mass-number - number; melting and boiling points in kelvin at 1 atmosphere
#   from standard reference tables (CRC Handbook values, rounded); "unknown" where never measured. The in-game uses come from the public Elements page.
# colour: the atom's usual colour in chemistry drawings (the Jmol CPK colours, Mt and below; none past Mt).
# ionise-ev: the energy to pull its outermost electron away, in electron-volts (the first ionisation energy, NIST Atomic
#   Spectra Database): a photon carrying more ionises it (ultraviolet and up for most atoms, visible light for the alkali metals).
# ion-charge and ionic-a: the charge it most often carries as an ion in minerals and salts, and its size as that ion in angstroms
#   (Shannon 1976, six neighbours round it): where it gives or takes electrons (an ionic bond, by its pull against its
#   neighbours'), it is that size; where it shares them, its bonding size; in between, in proportion (Pauling's ionic character).
# covalent-a: its bonding size in angstroms (Cordero et al. 2008, He to Cm; none measured past Cm): two atoms closer than
#   their two sizes together (with a fifth more room) are bonded. atomic-a: its own size, the calculated atomic radius in
#   angstroms (Clementi, Raimondi and Reinhardt 1967: H to Ba, Hf to Rn); where none is given its bonding size stands in.
# electronegativity: how hard it pulls on shared electrons (Pauling scale): two atoms that differ by about 1.7 or more do
#   not share their bonding electrons, one gives one over and they hold as ions (salt); less, they share a pair, nearer the
#   one that pulls harder.
# look: how a lump of it looks (its real colour) and shiny: a metal's shine; glow-colour: the colour the gas glows lit by
#   electricity (neon orange-red, argon violet, hydrogen pink). The families' colours on the periodic table are @family below.
# half-life and decays: for the elements with no stable form, how long half of a lump of the named isotope lasts and how it goes
#   (alpha: two protons and two neutrons flung out, the atom two places down the table; beta-minus: a neutron turns into a
#   proton, an electron flung out, one place up; electron-capture: a proton turns into a neutron, one place down; fission: the
#   nucleus splits in two); NUBASE2020 and the NNDC tables, rounded. The stable elements carry none: they last.
# made-in: where the element's atoms were forged, by the share of each source (the periodic table of nucleosynthesis, Johnson 2019): the big
#   bang (hydrogen, helium, some lithium), cosmic rays breaking heavier atoms (lithium, beryllium, boron), dying low-mass stars (carbon,
#   nitrogen, the s-process from strontium to lead), exploding massive stars (oxygen to zinc), exploding white dwarfs (the iron peak),
#   merging neutron stars (the r-process: the heavy rare earths, gold, platinum, thorium, uranium), uranium's and thorium's decay
#   (polonium to actinium, protactinium), the lab (technetium, promethium, everything past uranium). A body's card says where its elements were forged.
# abundance: how common it is in the cosmos, 12 + log of its atoms per hydrogen atom (hydrogen 12): the Sun's photosphere, else
#   CI meteorites (Asplund, Amarsi & Grevesse 2021, A&A 653, A141, Table 2); none where never measured (technetium, promethium,
#   polonium to protactinium, everything past uranium: none of it lasts in space).
# lines: its strongest visible lines, 3600 to 7600 angstroms in air, the ion giving each (I neutral, II once ionised, III twice)
#   and its strength against its own other lines (the NIST Handbook of Basic Atomic Spectroscopic Data, strong lines); none past
#   einsteinium, never measured.
# nebular-lines: the forbidden lines it glows in in a thin nebula, where an atom goes untouched long enough to give them (air
#   angstroms, the ion, the log of the density in electrons per cubic cm above which collisions take that level's energy
#   before it can shine, and its strength within its set): Keel, University of Alabama, emission-line table; the paired lines by
#   the fixed ratio of their shared upper level ([O III] 5007 to 4959 about 3, [N II] 6583 to 6548 about 3, [O I] 6300 to 6364
#   about 3); the auroral lines a few hundredths of the others.
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md · content/physics.unt

